{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target presents reduced sky view factor as 'the single mechanism' that makes the cross-city gap larger in some cities than others, but the sky view explanation is only the nocturnal cooling-rate modulator, not the dominant cause of the inter-city nighttime gap. Oke's empirical work shows UHI intensity scales most strongly with city size / population and canyon geometry together; isolating geometry alone is incomplete. More importantly, on a clear calm night the primary reason the city stays warmer is the difference in cooling rates driven by rural radiative loss vs urban storage release — the cross-city variation is better explained by total built-area extent and anthropogenic heat, which scale with population. The target's claim that trapped longwave 'dramatically slows the nocturnal cooling rate' assumes the urban surface continues to receive conducted stored heat throughout the night; if daytime heat storage fully dissipates early, geometry alone cannot sustain a several-degree gap, a possibility the target itself flags in its counterconditions but does not resolve.","premise":"Reduced sky view factor is not the single best explanation for why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than others; total urban thermal storage capacity and anthropogenic heat, which scale with city size/pop, are stronger controls.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8","quote":"the cross-city gap is larger in dense cities because deep street canyons with low sky view factors trap this released longwave radiation, preventing it from escaping to the sky"}]}]}